---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Status from '../../components/Status.astro';
import Video from '../../components/Video.astro';
import { url } from '../../data/url';
---
<Base slug="all-together/" title="All together: one evening" description="How every algorithm works together in one real conversation, and why follow-ups are cheap.">
  <header class="wrap phead">
    <p class="chapter">05 · All together</p>
    <h1 class="h1" data-split="lines" data-intro>One evening, every algorithm. Each message runs only what it needs.</h1>
    <div class="headrow" data-reveal="up" data-intro data-delay="0.3">
      <p class="lede muted">People don’t speak in one perfect sentence. They change their minds, ask side questions and start over. The card keeps the machine steady through all of it.</p>
      <Status s="designed" />
    </div>
  </header>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">The card</p>
    <div class="prose"><p>Behind every conversation sits one card: what to find, the rules that must hold (group size included), and the tastes for tonight. The AI keeps the card up to date from each message. Code reads the card and decides which stages need to run again. A change of taste re-runs ranking. A new group size re-runs the search. A side question runs only the explanation.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Fixed boxes, free content</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The card always has the same three boxes. What goes in them changes with every request, however vague or specific. The AI fills the boxes from the message, and code treats each box differently: musts become fences that remove places, likes become magnets that rank the rest. Group size counts as a must, because it is a limit that always holds.</p>
      <div class="tbl" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th>Box</th><th>What it holds</th><th>Veg dinner for 4</th><th>Something fun for 6 on Sunday</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Find</td><td>What to look for</td><td>Dinner, dessert on the way</td><td>An activity</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Must</td><td>Rules that always hold</td><td>Veg, 4 people, ₹500 each, home by 10</td><td>6 people, Sunday, no alcohol, close by</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Likes</td><td>Tastes, nice to have</td><td>South Indian, calm</td><td>Fun, plus what the notebook knows</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p>The first example is the “2 things to find, 4 rules, 1 taste” from the <a href={url('how-it-works/')}>Understand stage</a>.</p>
      <p>A request that can’t be written in these three boxes is outside the product, and that is where <a href={url('algorithms/saying-no/')}>saying no</a> applies.</p>
      <p><b>Card and tags are different things.</b> The card describes the <em>request</em>. Tags are a shared vocabulary that describes <em>places</em> (dessert, calm, South Indian). They meet in the Likes box: a taste on the card is a tag word, and code matches it to each place’s tags to score it. Musts are checked against facts about the place, not tags.</p>
      <p class="muted">The box names are settled. The full list of rule types that can go under Must (budget, diet, group size, deadline and so on) is still being written.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Watch the whole machine</p>
    <Video v="V13" title="The whole machine" />
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Asking for more</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>One more kind of follow-up: “show me more”. Nothing on the card changes, so the search, the fences and the ranking are all reused. Only the <a href={url('algorithms/mix-and-variety/')}>mix</a> runs again, leaving out the three already shown, and the facts are printed fresh for the new picks. “Give me 10 options” works the same way and returns a short list instead of three. Both are quick, because the search and the ranking are skipped.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tbl" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th>Message</th><th>Card</th><th>Runs again</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>“Make it somewhere livelier”</td><td>Taste changes</td><td>Ranking onwards</td></tr>
      <tr><td>“Is the thali place open late?”</td><td>No change</td><td>Only the explanation; the fact is already fetched</td></tr>
      <tr><td>“Show me more”</td><td>No change</td><td>Mix, route and facts, on the same shortlist</td></tr>
      <tr><td>“Give me 10 options”</td><td>Count becomes 10</td><td>Mix in list mode, then facts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>“Scrap it. We’re 10 now”</td><td>Group and area change</td><td>Everything, after one question</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Every algorithm, in order</p>
    <div class="tbl" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>On the example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 · Understand</td><td>Listen (AI)</td><td>Two things to find, four rules, one taste</td></tr><tr><td>1 · Understand</td><td><a href={url('algorithms/saying-no/')}>Saying no</a></td><td>Everything is allowed: serve</td></tr><tr><td>1 · Understand</td><td><a href={url('algorithms/asking/')}>Asking</a></td><td>Budget was said, the rest is known: no question</td></tr><tr><td>2 · Narrow</td><td><a href={url('algorithms/fences-and-magnets/')}>Fences</a></td><td>3,000 places → about 40</td></tr><tr><td>3 · Rank</td><td>Magnets, <a href={url('algorithms/memory/')}>memory</a>, <a href={url('algorithms/rest-period/')}>rest</a></td><td>40 scored; a place from last week would rest</td></tr><tr><td>3 · Rank</td><td><a href={url('algorithms/mix-and-variety/')}>Mix</a>, <a href={url('algorithms/wild-cards/')}>wild cards</a></td><td>Safe, stretch, wild; 6 dinners + 6 desserts go on</td></tr><tr><td>4 · Combine</td><td><a href={url('algorithms/route-and-arc/')}>Route and arc</a></td><td>Kaveri 7:30 → Malli 9:05 → home 9:40</td></tr><tr><td>5 · Check</td><td><a href={url('algorithms/facts/')}>Facts by code</a>, then explain (AI)</td><td>Facts printed from data; AI writes reasons</td></tr><tr><td>After</td><td>Learn</td><td>Sunday: “How was Kaveri?” updates the notebook</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </section>
</Base>
